QE = {1: "Now the nature of concealment, as it is told, let it be examined: from own nature the Supreme Lord touches the rule-bound and rule-free order.", 2: "Touching, he shines forth; thereby he is called self-willed. Having resorted to the rule of action and fruit — this Great Lord.", 3: "He ordains creation, preservation, destruction — the intermediate states; and again, in the great creation, in the endlessness of creation-destruction.", 4: "One, that God, universe-self, the Lord arrived at abandoning rule; and whatever intermediate creation — and stasis here too is unforced.", 5: "He does not abandon; this form standing here, niyati-abandoned: that. By niyati alone, when through the self-concealing procedure.", 6: "He enjoys suffering, delusion and the rest; then the action-fruit order — but having abandoned the karmic restraint, enveloped in suffering-delusion.", 7: "Desiring to shine forth, sits this one, in concealment disregardful; as from light's freedom, the awakened like the unawakened.", 8: "So sits, uncrossed yet as-if-crossed, behaving; as the awake one her fool-conduct performing even while hating it.", 9: "In the heart sits the fool — thus indeed of the awakened is the conduct; and the venerable Vidyā-lord said in the Mānastotra such as this.", 10: "Who on your teaching-path, initiated, falling through delusion into dissent: surely of them there is not your sun-appointment — can lotuses contract from sunbeams?", 11: "Known-and-knowable, standing in the creator's place though being, who going wrong from your path, they too: not right. Mostly of them, arisen from emblem-mind-and-the-rest, false awakening like snake-fat lamplight.", 12: "Since pierced, with lead foremost: now copper — that which again to its own nature would not return; nor drunk by earth-dwellers, that nectar of whom thirst-hunger-suffering-pains again herein.", 13: "Then this awakened conduct — mantra-conduct-worship-and-the-rest: hate-kindled inwardly burns him; burning is doubt indeed — for.", 14: "Nor is it his past action's might such that thereby thus he has come; indeed, what would that action be? Or why here would the procedure be the same as with the earlier?", 15: "Hence that, the Lord's will by which deluded so, seeks endless-time-felt suffering-vesselhood.", 16: "There too, if through will-variety both-here-and-beyond-natured: of suffering too there is division, made slow-or-swift, so.", 17: "Of Kāla, Kāma, Andhaka and the rest, of Paulastya-city dwellers, and of others: concealment — so much suffering, indeed hereafter too.", 18: "Another too is concealment, consisting in samaya-violation: which was said by the Lord in the venerable Ānandagahvara.", 19: "From samaya-violation, O Goddess: carrion-eaterhood for a hundred years; there too, through mild-intense-and-other-divisions, manifold is the course.", 20: "And through the Great Lord's freedom, though concealed this one himself, through another's door either comes back — again grace too, amply.", 21: "And from renewed grace, expiation-and-the-rest being performed, accordingly in initiation and the rest performed, he would be Śiva-natured.", 22: "Concealed — even the departed-breathed: kin, friends, teachers having resorted to, through śaktipāta, through initiations is graced.", 23: "There too, diversity of quick-slow-time and the rest: so comes śaktipāta — it, by which he comes to Śiva-nature.", 24: "Thus creation-maintenance-dissolution-concealment-grace: in the five, doership is Śiva-hood, of consciousness-self.", 25: "Proud of five-act-freedom-complete-selfhood: the yogin's worship-japa-meditation-yogas, let them stand united, ever-risen.", 26: "In magic-show business one should never delight; even Sadāśiva — which enjoyment is bondage — that too for the unfit.", 27: "Knowerness alone is Śiva-hood, is freedom: that is here called; like a potter, but, doership is not primary from His superintendence.", 28: "Thus having known, to be grasped, never at all, ever, refutation: 'I am Śiva' — if following my will, why does not the world — thus the doubt.", 29: "'Let them follow my will' — here in the I-knower, may it flash; Śiva or the Supreme Lord, body and the rest then fashioned.", 30: "Of Śiva, for now is this — the body — but this, so fashioned by you: what other bodiness of his? Then what would be a speakable state?", 31: "Told, and, in the Siddhasantāna venerable Ūrmi-Mahākula: wind-wandering-breath-scattering-and-the-rest-made toilers.", 32: "In frauds and the rest, who deluded — they, are deluded in the supreme state; everywhere with honor which even departure — for liberation.", 33: "Taught she in the essence-śāstras, as enjoyment-means arisen: if all-pervading God — tell! having departed — where will he go?", 34: "But if not all-pervading then, pot-like then he would be; the departure-rite-yoga — this partially — is taught.", 35: "In the partless Śiva-principle, but, how departure-fittingness? As from earth-and-the-rest to wind-end, by cliff-water-fire fasts.", 36: "Of the self joining in the void, likewise departure-procedure; therefore one should not make the soul depart out of longing for the supreme principle.", 37: "In the venerable Pūrvaśāstra, but told: whatever of departure the mark — not that, as a means to liberation, but for loss of enjoyment, from such desiring.", 38: "Perfected by japa-meditation-and-the-rest, through freedom, from śaktipāta, toward enjoyment detached — if thus, the body should abandon — quoted.", 39: "Even of self-willed death, what from Bhīṣma onward is heard, indeed: on arrival at enjoyment-distaste, approaching life's end.", 40: "By yoga-mantra-nectar-substance-boon-and-the-rest, a siddhi-partaking body: to abandon — for not otherwise is possible without the stated-sequence-practice.", 41: "Told, and, in the Mālinītantra, by the Supreme Lord, likewise, all — even, or else, enjoyment deeming ugly.", 42: "Thus-and-the-rest, by one who says: by all, the inner-reality-unseen reality, thus, teaching of one's own freedom in creation-and-the-rest duties.", 43: "Which very chief initiation should be of the disciple, Śiva-giving: told in the venerable Niśicāra, and, by Bhairavī energy, radiance.", 44: "Pervaded universe they behold, thought-abandoned-minded; but the thought-joined-minded: from body's fall Śiva reaches.", 45: "By outer-initiation-and-the-rest-practice, through conduct-samaya-constructs, thought-free just so now indeed: living-liberated, no doubt.", 46: "By thought made like the root-mass of the world-worn-out tree, inwardly, of the ultimate fire: may there be spark-fragments, even if at its end blazes intensely the pure-awakening-fire-mass.", 47: "Thus the initiation-approach — this, shown, scripturally approved."}
